-- DICT-SEMANTICS §1/§3, at a `requires` clause of TWO entries.
--
-- Companion to s3-nary-requires-goal-vector.mdk, which uses a SINGLE-entry
-- `requires`. That arity is the one at which a caller's route order and a
-- callee's dict-slot order cannot disagree, so a single-entry fixture is
-- structurally blind to anything ordering-related. This row raises the arity.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived from §1/§3, NOT captured from any engine):
--   `impl Sh (Box a) requires Dbg2 a, Ix a Char` pins the SECOND `Ix`
--   parameter to `Char`. At the goal `Sh (Box Int)` the head match binds
--   a ↦ Int, so the second obligation is the ground predicate `Ix Int Char`.
--   §3's matching set is `{ I | ∃φ. φ(head(I)) = C τ̄ }`, ONE φ against the
--   WHOLE vector: φ(`Ix Int Bool`) would need Bool = Char, so it is NOT in the
--   set; φ(`Ix Int Char`) = identity. The set is the singleton
--   {`impl Ix Int Char`} and `inst` selects it -- 222.
--   The first obligation `Dbg2 a` at a ↦ Int selects `impl Dbg2 Int`, so
--   `dbg2 5` is 5. 222 + 5 = 227.
--   Independently: the body calls `ix x 'z'` with `'z' : Char`, so no other
--   instance is type-correct at that call.
--
-- The wrong answer this row catches is 116 (= 111, the `Ix Int Bool` body,
-- + 5) -- which is what the PRE-FIX binary printed here. Declaration order
-- here is deliberately WRONG-ANSWER-FIRST (`Ix Int Bool` before
-- `Ix Int Char`), so a regression to the arg-0-only fallback reds this row.
-- Section 4 additionally reverses the two `Ix` blocks and asserts the answer
-- does not move.
--
-- 🚨 THIS ROW IS ORDER-SENSITIVE IN A DIMENSION IT DOES NOT ASSERT, AND THAT
-- IS DELIBERATE. Writing the requires clause the OTHER way round --
-- `requires Ix a Char, Dbg2 a` -- still prints 116 on this binary. That
-- residual is pinned as a MUST-FAIL at
-- test/must_fail_fixtures/1154-multi-entry-requires-decl-order-decides/, and
-- it is why this file must keep `Dbg2 a` first. Do NOT "tidy" the requires
-- clause into source order to match the body: that turns a green row red and
-- double-pins an open bug. The trigger is NOT a simple positional rule --
-- five measured orderings do not fit one -- see that fixture's claim.txt.
interface Ix a b where
  ix : a -> b -> Int

impl Ix Int Bool where
  ix _ _ = 111

impl Ix Int Char where
  ix _ _ = 222

interface Dbg2 a where
  dbg2 : a -> Int

impl Dbg2 Int where
  dbg2 n = n

data Box a = MkBox a

interface Sh a where
  sh : a -> Int

impl Sh (Box a) requires Dbg2 a, Ix a Char where
  sh (MkBox x) = ix x 'z' + dbg2 x

main = println (sh (MkBox 5))
